(3, 3, 3, 15, 'Unit - 1', 24, '[{\"number\":1,\"title\":\"K.L.N College of Engineering\",\"layout\":\"title\",\"bullets\":[\"Computer Science and Engineering\",\"Course Code: CS201\",\"Database Management Systems\",\"needs to trim that list to include only those students who have taken at least 60 credit hours. As expected\",\"Academic Year: 2026-27\",\"Semester: Odd\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Introduce Database Management Systems, Unit 1. Outline the session goals and expected outcomes.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Unit 1\"},{\"number\":2,\"title\":\"Learning Objectives\",\"layout\":\"objectives\",\"bullets\":[\"By the end of this unit, students should be able to:\",\"Explain database concepts and the purpose of a DBMS\",\"Model simple domains using entities, attributes, and relationships\",\"Distinguish primary keys, foreign keys, and integrity constraints\",\"Write basic SQL to query relational tables\",\"Explain why normalization reduces redundancy\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"State outcomes clearly. Link each objective to the topics that follow.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Unit 1\"},{\"number\":3,\"title\":\"Unit 1 Agenda\",\"layout\":\"content\",\"bullets\":[\"1. Data base System Applications\",\"2. Purpose of Database Systems\",\"3. View of Data – Data Abstraction –\",\"4. Instances and Schemas – data Models – the ER Model – Relational Model – Other Models –\",\"5. Database Languages – DDL – DML – database Access for applications Programs – data base Users\",\"6. and Administrator – Transaction Management – data base Architecture – Storage Manager – the\",\"7. Query Processor\",\"8. Data base design and ER diagrams – ER Model - Entities\"],\"speaker_notes\":\"Walk through the Unit 1 agenda and show how topics build on each other.\",\"unit_tag\":\"Unit 1\"},{\"title\":\"Data base System Applications\",\"layout\":\"content\",\"bullets\":[\"What it is: Data base System Applications — define the concept using standard Database Management Systems terminology.\",\"Why it matters: students use this